EXT. DRIVE-IN SCREEN TOWER - ROOFTOP - NIGHT

The back of an enormous white screen, scaffolded and creaking, rises above a field of parked cars. Light from the projection booth spills over the lot like milk. Beyond the fence, cornfields roll dark to the horizon, silent as sea.

On a ladder platform two stories up, PERPETUA "PEPPER" LINDQVIST, 15, sits with her knees pulled to her chin. Her denim jacket is covered in hand-sewn patches, each one a different band. A bag of stolen popcorn sits beside her, untouched. The wind ruffles her short hair. She is watching the lot below as if memorizing it.

Far down the lot, a car door slams. Laughter, faint. Somebody's radio drifts: a slow song.

A boot rings on the scaffold below. Pepper doesn't turn.

MILO ABARA, 16, climbs into view, lanky, glasses held to his face with tape at one hinge. He carries a thermos like a trophy. He sees her. Stops.

MILO
I thought nobody came up here.

PEPPER
Nobody does.

MILO
Then why are you up here?

PEPPER
Why are you?

He climbs the last rungs and sits, careful, at the far end of the platform, with the thermos between them like a border.

MILO
I work the snack bar. Third night. They don't pay me enough to be nice to people, so on my break I climb.

PEPPER
How'd you find it?

MILO
Fell off the ladder. Landed on the platform. Saw the view. Fell in love a little.

PEPPER
With the view.

MILO
With being too high to hear anyone ask for extra butter.

She almost smiles. Doesn't. He unscrews the thermos, pours something steaming into the cap, and holds it out.

MILO (CONT'D)
Hot chocolate. It's bad.

PEPPER
Bad how?

MILO
Powdered. Lumpy. But hot.

She takes it. Drinks. Makes a face. Drinks again.

PEPPER
It's terrible.

MILO
Told you.

PEPPER
My brother used to make it like this. Used to burn it on purpose because he said the burnt part was the good part.

MILO
Where is he?

PEPPER
Army. Fort something. He sends postcards with no words. Just stamps.

MILO
Stamps?

PEPPER
He picks the weirdest ones. Last time it was a stamp of a moth. I think it means he's okay.

MILO
Or he misses you.

PEPPER
Or the post office only had moths.

Milo laughs, too loud. The sound carries. She shushes him, laughing herself, and they both look down.

Below, a flashlight beam sweeps the fence line, bored, unhurried.

They both freeze, shoulders touching. Pepper puts a finger to her lips. Milo nods, eyes huge behind his taped glasses.

The beam passes. Slides along the scaffold. Pauses at the platform edge. Passes on.

They breathe again.

PEPPER
(whispering)
That's twice you've almost gotten me arrested.

MILO
(whispering)
It's the first time we've met.

PEPPER
You know what I mean.

MILO
I really don't.

PEPPER
Fine. Next time, bring better chocolate.

MILO
There's going to be a next time?

PEPPER
You tell me. You work here.

Milo goes red to the ears. He fumbles with the thermos cap. Drops it. They both lunge for it and knock heads. Both freeze, rubbing their foreheads, and then both start laughing, silent shaking laughter, hands clamped over their mouths.

Down in the lot, the movie's climactic music swells. A kiss, perhaps, on the big white screen they can't see from behind. A car horn honks, a cheer, a ripple of applause.

Pepper wipes her eyes. The laughter has turned into something else, quieter. She looks at the dark cornfields.

PEPPER (CONT'D)
My mom's selling the house in June.

MILO
Where are you going?

PEPPER
Don't know yet. Somewhere with a lot of stamps, I guess.

MILO
You could stay.

PEPPER
With who?

Milo looks at his sneakers. At his hands. At the thermos cap in them.

MILO
I don't know. Me, maybe. Eventually.

PEPPER
You're sixteen.

MILO
Sixteen and a half.

PEPPER
That's not a plan, Milo.

MILO
It's the start of one.

She looks at him for a long moment. In the light from the booth, her face is open, young, unguarded.

PEPPER
Okay. A start.

She takes the thermos cap from his hand, pours a last splash of terrible chocolate, and clinks it against his.

PEPPER (CONT'D)
To moths.

MILO
To moths.

They drink. The song on the radio ends. Another begins.
